\id 2PE - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Peter
\toc1 The Second Letter of Peter to Those Allotted a Faithfulness Equal in Honor to Ours
\toc2 2 Peter
\toc3 2Pe
\mt1 2 Peter

\imt Introduction
\ip This is the second letter Symeon Peter sent to the same readers (3:1). The first went to assemblies scattered through the Roman provinces of northern and western Asia Minor — Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia, in what is now Turkey. They are largely people of the nations rather than Jews by birth, and the first thing this letter tells them is that what they have been given is worth exactly what the apostles' own is worth. The date is the middle of the first century.
\ip Peter writes as a man with very little time left. He says plainly that his tent is about to be taken down and that the Lord himself had told him so (1:14). That places the letter in the oldest form of last words there is: the departing leader — Jacob, Moses, Joshua, Samuel, David — gathering those he is leaving to tell them what is coming and what to do about it. He is not opening new subjects. He is making sure that what they already know will outlive him.
\ip Two dangers provoked it. The first is teachers rising inside the assemblies themselves, exactly as false prophets once rose inside Israel — men who take money for it, who promise freedom while living as slaves of their own appetites, and who work hardest on the newest and least settled believers. The second is mockery of the promise itself: the first generation has died, nothing visible has changed, and men are asking aloud where the promised coming has got to.
\ip Against both, the letter argues one thing from beginning to end. God has already given everything needed; what remains is to build on it and keep building. The alternative is not standing still but sliding back — and sliding back, after having known the way, leaves a man worse off than he was before he ever heard of it.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Symeon Peter\f + \fr 1:1 \ft \tl Symeōn\tl* is the Hebrew form of the name, not the Greek \tl Simōn\tl* used nearly everywhere else. In the New Testament it stands only here and on James's lips in Acts 15:14. It is a patriarch's name — Jacob's second son.\f*, a slave\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl doulos\tl*, which renders Hebrew \tl ebed\tl* in the Greek scriptures — the title borne by Moses, Joshua, David, and the prophets as men bound to God's own service (Deuteronomy 34:5; Joshua 24:29; 2 Samuel 7:5; Amos 3:7). It raises a man rather than lowering him.\f* and one sent\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*: an emissary who carries the full authority of the one who sent him.\f* of Jesus the Christ, to those who have been allotted\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl lanchanō\tl*, to obtain by lot — the word used of the tribes receiving their portions of the land (Joshua 14:2). What these readers hold was handed out to them; they did not work it up.\f* a faithfulness equal in honor with ours \add [i.e. not some lesser version of what we of Israel were given, but the same thing at the same worth]\add*, in the righteousness\f + \fr 1:1 \ft In Israel's prophets God's righteousness is not an attribute he holds but a thing he does — his covenant faithfulness going into action to save (Isaiah 46:13; 51:5; 56:1).\f* of our God and Rescuer Jesus the Christ\f + \fr 1:1 \ft A single article governs both "God" and "Rescuer" in the Greek, so both land on one person. \tl Sōtēr\tl*, "Rescuer," is God's own title in the Greek scriptures: "a righteous God and a Rescuer; there is none besides me" (Isaiah 45:21; Hosea 13:4).\f*:
\v 2 may favor and peace be multiplied to you in the full knowing\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl epignōsis\tl*, the letter's governing word (1:2, 1:3, 1:8, 2:20). Not information held about God but knowledge that has been taken in, acted on, and lived — the thing the false teachers of chapter 2 will counterfeit.\f* of God and of Jesus our Lord.
\s1 Everything Needed Has Already Been Given
\p
\v 3 Seeing that his divine power has given us as a gift everything that goes toward life and reverence for God \add [i.e. nothing you still need is missing, and nothing further is coming]\add*, through the full knowing of the one who called us by his own glory and excellence\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl aretē\tl*. Applied to God in Israel's scriptures it does not mean abstract moral quality but his demonstrable, praiseworthy acts — the deeds his people are to declare (Isaiah 42:12; 43:21; 1 Peter 2:9).\f*,
\v 4 through which he has given us as a gift the precious and greatest promises, so that through these you may become sharers of a divine nature\f + \fr 1:4 \ft The sentence itself sets the terms: a divine nature over against decay. To share it is to share in what does not rot — his holiness, his permanence — as far as a creature can. The same demand stands behind "be holy, for I am holy" (Leviticus 19:2; 1 Peter 1:16).\f*, having fled the decay that is in the world through craving \add [i.e. wanting is the engine the rot runs on]\add*.
\s1 What You Must Supply on Top of It
\p
\v 5 And for this very reason, bringing all eagerness to bear, in your faithfulness supply\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl epichorēgeō\tl*, the word for what a wealthy citizen did when he outfitted and trained a chorus for the city's festivals at his own cost — not a contribution but lavish provision. What follows is a chain: each quality is the ground the next grows out of, and none is left behind when the next arrives.\f* excellence; and in the excellence, knowing;
\v 6 and in the knowing, mastery of yourself; and in the mastery of yourself, endurance; and in the endurance, reverence for God\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl eusebeia\tl*, a word for the whole bearing of a life turned toward God, not for acts of worship alone. It stands here in the middle of the chain, not at its head: the deep reverence comes after faithfulness has been tested, not before.\f*;
\v 7 and in the reverence for God, brotherly love; and in the brotherly love, love.
\s1 Make Your Calling and Choosing Firm
\p
\v 8 For these things, when they are yours and are increasing \add [i.e. not merely present but growing — a standstill will not do]\add*, make you neither idle nor without fruit toward the full knowing of our Lord Jesus the Christ.
\v 9 For the one in whom these things are not present is blind, squinting \add [i.e. able to make out only what is close at hand, and nothing at all of what is coming]\add*, having taken on forgetfulness of the cleansing of his old sins \add [i.e. living as though he had never been washed]\add*.
\v 10 Therefore, brothers, be all the more eager to make your calling and choosing firm\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl bebaios\tl*, the word used of confirming a contract or a sale so that it holds in law. The calling and the choosing are God's doing; the ratifying is theirs. They are not asked to produce it but to make good on it.\f*; for if you do these things you will never stumble at any time.
\v 11 For in this way the entrance into the everlasting kingdom of our Lord and Rescuer Jesus the Christ will be richly supplied\f + \fr 1:11 \ft The same verb as in verse 5, now turned around on them: they supply excellence lavishly, and lavishly the way in is supplied to them. The entrance will not be grudging or narrow.\f* to you.
\s1 Why I Am Writing Now: My Death Is Near
\p
\v 12 Therefore I intend always to remind you of these things, even though you know them and have been made steady in the truth that is with you \add [— my trouble is not that you were never told, but that a man forgets]\add*.
\v 13 And I count it right, as long as I am in this tent\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Greek \tl skēnōma\tl*, a tent — the dwelling of people on the move, pitched for the journey and struck when it ends. Paul uses the same figure of the body in 2 Corinthians 5:1.\f*, to wake you up with a reminder,
\v 14 knowing that the putting off of my tent is coming quickly, just as our Lord Jesus the Christ also made it clear to me\f + \fr 1:14 \ft John 21:18-19, where the risen Jesus tells Peter that when he is old another will bind him and take him where he does not wish to go — indicating, John adds, by what kind of death he would glorify God.\f*.
\v 15 And I will be eager that at any time, after my going out\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Greek \tl exodos\tl*, the word for Israel's going out of Egypt, and the word Moses and Elijah used on the mountain of what Jesus was about to accomplish at Jerusalem (Luke 9:31). Set it beside the "entrance" of verse 11: the shepherd goes out so that the flock may come in.\f*, you will be able to call these things to mind.
\s1 We Saw His Magnificence Ourselves
\p
\v 16 For it was not by following cleverly devised tales \add [i.e. stories worked up to sound convincing]\add* that we made known to you the power and coming\f + \fr 1:16 \ft Greek \tl parousia\tl*, the standard word among the earliest assemblies for the arrival of Christ in glory — the very thing the mockers of 3:4 will say has failed to happen.\f* of our Lord Jesus the Christ; we saw his magnificence with our own eyes \add [— I was on that mountain, and James and John with me]\add*.
\v 17 For he received honor and glory from God the Father when a voice like this was carried to him by the Magnificent Glory\f + \fr 1:17 \ft A way of naming God by his splendor rather than by his name, in keeping with Jewish reverence for the name itself.\f*: "This is my Son, my Beloved; in him I have taken delight."\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Three strands twisted into one sentence: the king addressed as God's son in Psalm 2:7; the chosen servant God delights in at Isaiah 42:1; and the beloved son of Genesis 22:2, the one taken up the mountain to be offered.\f*
\v 18 And we ourselves heard this voice carried out of heaven, being with him on the holy mountain\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Israel's holy mountains are Sinai, where God spoke out of the cloud, and Zion, where he set his king (Exodus 24:15-18; Psalm 2:6). This is a third such place — where heaven opened, the voice came, and the glory was seen resting on a man.\f*.
\s1 Where Prophecy Comes From
\p
\v 19 And we have the prophetic word, firmer still \add [i.e. what the prophets said, now with what we saw standing behind it]\add*, and you do well to hold to it as to a lamp shining in a murky place, until the day breaks through and the morning star\f + \fr 1:19 \ft Greek \tl phōsphoros\tl*, the star that rises just ahead of the sun and announces that the night is over. Behind it stands Balaam's oracle: "a star shall come forth from Jacob, a scepter shall rise from Israel" (Numbers 24:17), read in that century as a word about the coming king.\f* rises in your hearts,
\v 20 knowing this first: that no prophecy of scripture comes about by anyone's own explaining \add [i.e. no prophet ever worked out the meaning of things for himself and then announced it]\add*.
\v 21 For prophecy was never carried along by the will of a man, but men spoke from God as they were carried along\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Greek \tl pherō\tl*, the word used of a ship driven before the wind (Acts 27:15, 17). The prophets are the vessel; the Spirit is what moves them.\f* by the Holy Spirit.

\c 2
\s1 False Teachers Will Come Up Among You
\p
\v 1 But there were also false prophets among the people \add [i.e. inside Israel, not outside it]\add*, as there will also be false teachers among you, who will smuggle in\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Greek \tl pareisagō\tl*, to bring something in alongside, by a side door — the word for slipping in what has no right to be there.\f* factions of destruction, even denying the Master who bought them\f + \fr 2:1 \ft \tl Despotēs\tl* is the owner, the one with absolute title; \tl agorazō\tl* is to buy in the market, the word for the purchase of a slave (1 Corinthians 6:20; Revelation 5:9). Behind it stands the Song of Moses: "Is not he your Father who bought you?" — asked of the very people who then abandoned him (Deuteronomy 32:6, 15).\f* \add [— bought outright, and turning on the one who paid]\add*, bringing on themselves swift destruction.
\v 2 And many will follow their shameless ways, and because of them the way of the truth will be blasphemed \add [i.e. outsiders will judge the whole road by the men staggering along it]\add*.
\v 3 And in their greed they will make merchandise of you with molded words\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Greek \tl plastos\tl*, moulded, shaped to fit — teaching made to order for what will sell. The verb for "make merchandise" is a trader's word: the assembly is the market and its people are the stock.\f*. Their judgment from long ago is not idle, and their destruction does not doze.
\s1 God Has Judged Before, and Rescued Before
\p
\v 4 For if God did not spare angels when they sinned, but with chains of gloom cast them down to Tartaros\f + \fr 2:4 \ft \tl Tartaros\tl* is the pit below the pit in Greek telling, where the rebel Titans were bound. The verb built from it is found nowhere else in the Greek scriptures. What is being told is the old account of the angels who crossed the boundary set for them (Genesis 6:1-4), bound in darkness until the day of judgment — as 1 Enoch 6-16 and Jude 6 also tell it.\f* and handed them over to be kept for judgment;
\v 5 and did not spare the ancient world, but guarded Noah, a herald of righteousness\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Genesis does not call Noah a preacher. Jewish teaching had long held that he did not merely live rightly while the world ran on, but called that world to account and was refused.\f*, the eighth \add [i.e. one of eight and no more — himself, his wife, three sons and their wives]\add*, when he brought a flood on the world of ungodly men;
\v 6 and turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah to ashes he condemned them to ruin, having set them as an example of what is coming for the ungodly;
\v 7 and he rescued righteous Lot\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Genesis tells Lot's story with very little to commend him — he chose the plain by Sodom, offered his own daughters to the mob, and had to be dragged out by the hand. The point being made is not Lot's record but God's ability to pick the righteous man out of a doomed city and get him out of it.\f*, worn down by the way of life of lawless men in their shamelessness —
\v 8 for by what that righteous man saw and heard, living among them day after day, he tortured his righteous self with their lawless deeds \add [i.e. it cost him something every day simply to keep his eyes open there]\add* —
\v 9 then the Lord knows how to rescue those who revere him out of testing, and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for a day of judgment,
\v 10 and above all those who walk after flesh in the craving of defilement and who despise lordship. Bold, pleased with themselves, they do not tremble as they blaspheme glories\f + \fr 2:10 \ft "Glories" (Greek \tl doxai\tl*) is used here of exalted beings of the unseen world — powers before whom a man ought to be afraid to open his mouth.\f*,
\v 11 where angels, though greater in strength and power, do not carry a blaspheming judgment against them before the Lord\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Jude 9 gives the case: the archangel Michael, contending with the devil over the body of Moses, did not dare pronounce a reviling sentence but said only, "The Lord rebuke you." What the strongest angel will not presume to do, these men do at table.\f*.
\s1 What These Men Are Like
\p
\v 12 But these men, like animals without reason\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Greek \tl aloga\tl*, "without \tl logos\tl*" — lacking the reasoning that separates a man from a beast. Not how they were made, but what they have made of themselves.\f*, born by nature for capture and destruction, blaspheming in matters they know nothing about, will also be destroyed in their destruction,
\v 13 wronged as the wages of wrongdoing \add [i.e. paid out in the coin they themselves minted]\add*. They count it a pleasure to revel in the daytime \add [i.e. not even waiting for dark to cover it]\add*. Stains and blemishes, they revel in their deceits\f + \fr 2:13 \ft The Greek word for "deceits" (\tl apatais\tl*) differs by one letter from the word for the love feasts (\tl agapais\tl*), and some copies read that instead. Either way the picture is the same: the corruption is seated at the common table.\f* as they feast along with you \add [i.e. at your own gatherings, sharing your food]\add*,
\v 14 with eyes full of an adulteress \add [i.e. no woman crosses their sight but they take her that way]\add* and never resting from sin, baiting\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Greek \tl deleazō\tl*, the word for baiting a hook or setting a lure in a trap. It is repeated at verse 18, where the intended catch is named.\f* unsteady lives, with a heart trained in greed — children of a curse\f + \fr 2:14 \ft A Semitism: "children of" a thing means men marked out by it, men whose character it is.\f*.
\s1 They Have Taken Balaam's Road
\p
\v 15 Leaving behind the straight way \add [— they were on it once; this is a departure, not an absence]\add*, they were led astray, following the way of Balaam son of Bosor\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Numbers 22-24: hired by Moab to curse Israel, Balaam blessed it instead — but Numbers 31:16 records that he afterward showed Moab how to bring Israel down at Peor by immorality and idolatry. By the first century he stood for the prophet who knows the truth and sells it.\f*, who loved the wages of wrongdoing
\v 16 but was rebuked for his own lawbreaking: a voiceless beast of burden spoke with a man's voice and stopped the prophet's madness \add [— the dumb animal saw what the man with God's word in his mouth could not]\add*.
\p
\v 17 These are springs without water \add [i.e. you come to them thirsty and there is nothing in them]\add* and mists driven by a squall, for whom the gloom of darkness has been kept \add [— the same darkness that holds the angels of verse 4]\add*.
\v 18 For speaking swollen words of emptiness, they bait — with cravings of flesh, with shameless acts — those who are barely escaping from the ones who live in error \add [i.e. new people, only just out of the life they came from and not yet clear of it]\add*,
\v 19 promising them freedom while they themselves are slaves of decay; for by whatever a man is beaten, to that he has been enslaved \add [i.e. whatever defeats you owns you]\add*.
\s1 Worse at the End Than at the Start
\p
\v 20 For if, having fled the defilements of the world by the full knowing of our Lord and Rescuer Jesus the Christ\f + \fr 2:20 \ft The verb for fleeing and the word for full knowing are the same ones used of the readers themselves at 1:2-4. Nothing weaker is claimed for these men than for those being warned.\f*, they are again tangled up in them and beaten, the last things have become worse for them than the first\f + \fr 2:20 \ft The Lord's own words about the swept and garnished house and the seven worse spirits: "the last state of that man becomes worse than the first" (Matthew 12:45; Luke 11:26).\f*.
\v 21 For it would have been better for them not to have fully known the way of righteousness than, having fully known it, to turn back from the holy commandment handed on to them \add [i.e. the instruction they received from us, passed down and not invented]\add*.
\v 22 What the true proverb says has happened to them: "A dog turned back to its own vomit," and, "A sow washed clean, to wallowing in the mud."\f + \fr 2:22 \ft The first is Proverbs 26:11. The second is not in Israel's scriptures but was a well-worn saying of the region. Note what the second says and does not say: the sow was genuinely washed. What is described is a return, not a fraud.\f*

\c 3
\s1 Remember the Prophets and the Ones Sent
\p
\v 1 This is now the second letter\f + \fr 3:1 \ft The first is 1 Peter, sent to the same assemblies in Asia Minor (1 Peter 1:1). They are meant to be read as a pair.\f* I am writing to you, beloved, and in both of them I am waking up your clear mind\f + \fr 3:1 \ft Greek \tl eilikrinēs\tl*, said of something held up to sunlight and found to have nothing mixed into it. Their thinking is not corrupt; the danger is that it goes drowsy.\f* with a reminder,
\v 2 to remember the words spoken beforehand by the holy prophets, and the commandment of the Lord and Rescuer given through the ones sent to you \add [i.e. those of us who first brought you the message]\add*.
\s1 Mockers Who Ask Where the Promise Is
\p
\v 3 Know this first: that in the last of the days\f + \fr 3:3 \ft The prophets' own phrase for the closing stretch of history before God acts (Isaiah 2:2; Daniel 10:14; Hosea 3:5). Peter had already declared at Pentecost that these were those days (Acts 2:17).\f* mockers will come with their mocking, walking after their own cravings \add [— the objection is not an argument they have reasoned out; it is one their appetites require]\add*
\v 4 and saying, "Where is the promise of his coming? For ever since the fathers fell asleep\f + \fr 3:4 \ft "Fell asleep" was the ordinary way the assemblies spoke of a believer's death. "The fathers" here are most likely the first generation of believers, who received the promise and died without seeing it kept.\f*, everything goes on just as it has from the beginning of creation."
\s1 The World Has Been Undone Once Already
\p
\v 5 For, wanting it so, this escapes them \add [i.e. they have not missed it; they have declined to see it]\add*: that heavens existed long ago, and an earth standing together out of water and through water by the word of God,
\v 6 and that through these the world of that time was flooded with water and destroyed \add [— so much for everything going on as it always has]\add*.
\v 7 But by the same word the heavens and the earth that are now have been stored up\f + \fr 3:7 \ft Greek \tl thēsaurizō\tl*, the word for laying up treasure. Every further day the world runs is not evidence that it will last, but evidence that the appointed day has not yet come. Fire as the instrument of that day runs through the prophets: Deuteronomy 32:22; Isaiah 66:15-16; Malachi 4:1.\f* for fire, kept for a day of judgment and destruction of ungodly men.
\s1 Not Slow — Long-Suffering
\p
\v 8 But do not let this one thing escape you, beloved: that one day with the Lord is like a thousand years, and a thousand years like one day\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Psalm 90:4: "For a thousand years in your sight are like yesterday when it passes by." The psalm is Moses', and its subject is exactly this — the gulf between how long a thing feels to us and what it is to God.\f*.
\v 9 The Lord is not slow about the promise, as some count slowness, but he is long-suffering\f + \fr 3:9 \ft Greek \tl makrothymeō\tl*, literally "long-tempered," the opposite of short-tempered. It is the word of Exodus 34:6, where God declares his own character to Moses: "slow to anger, abounding in loyal love." The waiting is not neglect; it is the oldest thing Israel knew about him.\f* toward you, not wanting any to be destroyed but all to come to a turning back \add [i.e. a change of mind that turns the whole life around with it]\add*.
\s1 The Day of the Lord Comes Like a Thief
\p
\v 10 But the day of the Lord will come like a thief\f + \fr 3:10 \ft The Lord's own image (Matthew 24:43-44), passed on through the assemblies (1 Thessalonians 5:2; Revelation 16:15). A thief's whole method is that no one is expecting him.\f*, in which the heavens will pass away with a roar, and the elemental things\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Greek \tl stoicheia\tl*, the basic constituents out of which the physical world is put together.\f* will be dissolved as they burn, and the earth and the works in it will be found \add [i.e. laid bare, with nothing left covering anything]\add*.\f + \fr 3:10 \ft "Will be found" is the reading of the oldest copies; others read "will be burned up," and one "will not be found." The harder reading is likely the original, and it says something the others lose: the fire does not only destroy, it exposes. Compare 1 Corinthians 3:13.\f*
\p
\v 11 Since all these things are being dissolved in this way, what sort of people must you be — in holy ways of life and acts of reverence for God,
\v 12 looking for and hastening\f + \fr 3:12 \ft Greek \tl speudō\tl* can mean either to hurry a thing along or to be eager for it. If the first, then how these readers live bears on when the day comes — the same connection Peter draws in Acts 3:19-21.\f* the coming of the day of God, because of which the heavens will be set on fire and dissolved, and the elemental things melt as they burn?
\v 13 But according to his promise we are looking for new heavens and a new earth\f + \fr 3:13 \ft Isaiah 65:17 and 66:22. The word for "new" is \tl kainos\tl*, new in kind and quality rather than merely the next one off the line.\f*, in which righteousness makes its home \add [i.e. lives there permanently, rather than visiting a world that resents it]\add*.
\s1 Be Found Without Stain
\p
\v 14 Therefore, beloved, since you are looking for these things, be eager to be found by him without stain and without blemish\f + \fr 3:14 \ft These are the two words used of the false teachers at 2:13 — "stains and blemishes" — turned exactly around. What those men are is what these readers must not be.\f*, in peace.
\v 15 And count the long-suffering of our Lord to be rescue \add [i.e. every day he waits is a day someone can still turn]\add* — just as our beloved brother Paul also wrote to you, according to the wisdom given him,
\v 16 as he does in all his letters when he speaks in them about these things. In them there are some things hard to understand, which the untaught and the unsteady twist\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl strebloō\tl*, to wrench out of shape — the word used of stretching something on a rack.\f*, as they do the rest of the scriptures too\f + \fr 3:16 \ft "The rest of the scriptures" places Paul's letters in the same class as the sacred writings. This is the earliest surviving statement that does so.\f*, to their own destruction.
\s1 Guard Yourselves and Keep Growing
\p
\v 17 You then, beloved, knowing this beforehand \add [— you cannot say afterward that you were not told]\add*, guard yourselves, so that you are not carried away with the error of lawless men and fall from your own steadiness \add [i.e. from ground you are genuinely standing on]\add*.
\v 18 But grow in favor and in the knowing of our Lord and Rescuer Jesus the Christ \add [— there is no third option between growing and being swept off]\add*. To him be the glory both now and to the day of the age\f + \fr 3:18 \ft A phrase found nowhere else. It gathers up the day of judgment (3:7), the day of the Lord (3:10), and the day of God (3:12) into the one day that has no further day after it.\f*. Amen.
